Reversing scanned Micronix man pages back into source
=====================================================

These pages were recovered from the hardcopy in

    extra/docs/Micronix_Operating_System_1.61_Jun83.pdf

by reading the Acrobat text layer and re-marking it up by hand.  This
file records the format, the house conventions, and where the process
goes wrong, so the same thing can be done again when the library
documentation is scanned.

The tools live in tools/ and are described at the end.


1.  The format is form(1), not troff
------------------------------------

/bin/man is a Whitesmiths binary.  Its strings give the whole story:
it looks under /usr/man/man<section>, falls back to /usr/help/, and
formats by exec'ing form.  There are no macros - a page calls the
formatter's primitive requests directly - so nothing from the nroff
-man world applies here.

form(1) is documented in man1/form.1 and implements sixteen requests:

    .bd n   bold the next n lines      .nf     no fill
    .bp n   begin page n               .pl n   page length (66)
    .br     break                      .rm n   right margin (60)
    .ce n   centre the next n lines    .sp n   space n lines
    .fi     fill (the default)         .ta n   tab
    .fo T   footer                     .ti n   temporary indent
    .he T   header                     .ul n   underline next n lines
    .in n   indent                     .ls n   line spacing

All are used across the 183 original pages except .pl and .ls, which
are documented and never invoked.

A line whose first character is a period is a command.  Five of the
original pages have prose that begins with one and loses a line as a
result - see link.1 line 111 (".data sections."), sh.1, edit.1,
update.1 (".BD", which form does not match because it is upper case)
and Intro.2.  Watch for this when reversing.


2.  The page skeleton
---------------------

Four lines of furniture, then alternating section headings and bodies:

    .he "name (n)                     4/6/83                    name (n)"
    .fo "                               -#-"
    .in 5
    .rm 65

The .he text is 65 columns wide with the page name at both ends.  The
date is NOT exactly centred - null.4 splits 20/22 around it - and 19
of the 183 originals pad with tabs rather than spaces.  Every page
carries -#- in the footer; # is the page number.

Order varies: 165 pages write .he .fo .in .rm, 12 write .in .rm .he
.fo, and a handful do something else.  The first is the majority.

A section heading is five lines:

    .in -5
    .bd
    FUNCTION
    .in +5
    .br

.bd bolds exactly the one line after it, which is why a bolded word
in running prose sits on a line of its own.  The final section is
never closed - 134 of the originals end at indent 10.

A minority idiom (13 pages, e.g. cal.1) uses base .in 10 with .ti -5
to outdent headings instead of the .in -5 / .in +5 pair.  Prefer the
majority form.


3.  The heading vocabulary is per section
-----------------------------------------

    man1    PROGRAM (89) or NAME (13), USAGE or SYNTAX, FUNCTION or
            DESCRIPTION, EXAMPLE(S), SEE ALSO, NOTES, FILES, BUGS
    man2    NAME, C, FUNCTION, RETURNS, ASSEMBLER, RETURNS
    man3    SUBROUTINE(S), C, FUNCTION(S), RETURNS, ERRORS, NOTES,
            ALSO READ
    man4    DEVICE, FUNCTION or DESCRIPTION
    man5    FILE, FUNCTION, FILES, SEE ALSO

man2 documents every call twice, C then ASSEMBLER, each with its own
RETURNS - the assembler half carries the syscall number and register
contract:

    (read = 3)
    (file descriptor in hl)
    sys; read; buffer; nbytes
    (number of bytes read in hl)

IMPORTANT: the PRINTED manual says NAME everywhere.  The sources do
not.  null.4 carries the same date as the printed page and still says
DEVICE; rc.5 says FILE.  So "NAME" in a scan of section 3, 4 or 5 is
a typesetting normalisation - use the source spelling above.


4.  What a hand reversal gets wrong
-----------------------------------

Measured by reversing null.4, rc.5, chdir.2 and alloc.3 blind - all
four have header dates matching the source on disk, so every
difference is reconstruction error rather than a revision change.

  a. .bd TAKES A COUNT and the originals lean on it.  41 of 183 pages
     use it; .bd 2 appears 45 times, .bd 3 sixteen.  A declaration
     block is written

         .bd 2
         char *alloc(size)
         unsigned size;

     not as alternating .bd and .br.  This is the biggest single
     structural tell.

  b. EVERY IDENTIFIER IS BOLDED IN PROSE, not just command names -
     parameters too.  alloc.3 bolds size, ptr, nelem and elsize
     wherever they are mentioned.  The originals run 14.2 .bd per 100
     lines; a first pass that bolds only command names runs about 7.5.

  c. A PARAGRAPH BREAK IS A BLANK LINE, not .sp.  The tree uses blank
     lines over .sp about two to one.  But .sp IS used between
     declaration groups inside a C section, and .sp 2 before a
     section heading.  Do not convert blindly.

  d. LINES ARE FILLED LONGER than they look - the originals wrap
     around 70 columns, not 55.  form re-fills anyway, so this is
     cosmetic, but it is what makes a page look hand-typed.

  e. ONE INDENTED LINE IS .ti +5, not an .in +5 / .in -5 pair.  This
     is how null.4 sets its example:

         .in +5
         .ti +5
         .br
         cat file > /dev/null

     Bracketing every short example the other way doubles the .in
     count - 17.9 per page against the originals' 8.9 - while .ti
     stays flat at about 2.3 either way.  Reserve the pair for a
     block of several lines.

  f. Trailing whitespace is everywhere in the originals (a bolded
     word is often "Alloc " with a trailing space, headings are
     padded).  Harmless, and not worth reproducing deliberately.


5.  OCR damage to expect
------------------------

The 2011 Acrobat text layer is poor in predictable ways.  Seen
repeatedly in this batch:

    l <-> 1     cpl for cp1, mu1tio, 1pr, de1char, initfa1ize
    I <-> |     "man edit I split" for a pipe
    I <-> l     Is for ls, In for ln, hI for hl
    O <-> 0     pprO, diabO, "numbered O", "returns O"
    e <-> c     alloe, ealloe, ehar
    ~           substituted for almost anything: al~rm, ea~h, thir~
    •••         for ...
    .           inserted mid-word: "structur.e", "set .. could"

Section headers scan badly too: "l~b (1) OPTION" and
"printers (41" both defeated the page-boundary detector and let one
page absorb the next.  Check the tail of every extracted page.

Some pages have NO text layer at all (page 505, for instance).  Use
Read on the PDF with a page range to look at those directly.


6.  Tools
---------

    tools/index.py      list every man page in a scan's text layer,
                        allowing lettered subsections (3s, 3m, 3c),
                        and diff that against what is already on disk
    tools/extract.py    cut one page out of the text layer, dropping
                        running headers and footers
    tools/check.py      validate reversed pages: only real form(1)
                        requests, .he exactly 65 columns
    tools/compare.py    request mix per page, reversed vs original,
                        to see how far off the house style you are

Run index.py first to find what is missing, extract.py per page, mark
up by hand, then check.py and compare.py.

The fidelity test is worth repeating on any new batch: pick a page
that exists BOTH in the scan and on disk AND whose header dates
agree, reverse it without looking at the source, and diff.  That is
how everything in section 4 was found.
